L257 -- what the weak-lensing RAR actually measures

    four stellar-mass bins, [8, 8, 8, 8] points each beyond R = 0.3 Mpc

V1 -- the deep branch's slope is the ESD power law re-plotted
    bin 1 (log M* = 9.40): ESD log-slope = -1.013 +/- 0.203 -> gamma = 2.013 -> predicted relation slope (gamma-1)/2 = 0.506 +/- 0.101
    bin 2 (log M* = 10.45): ESD log-slope = -1.215 +/- 0.114 -> gamma = 2.215 -> predicted relation slope (gamma-1)/2 = 0.607 +/- 0.057
    bin 3 (log M* = 10.70): ESD log-slope = -1.060 +/- 0.080 -> gamma = 2.060 -> predicted relation slope (gamma-1)/2 = 0.530 +/- 0.040
    bin 4 (log M* = 10.90): ESD log-slope = -1.084 +/- 0.076 -> gamma = 2.084 -> predicted relation slope (gamma-1)/2 = 0.542 +/- 0.038
    combined prediction from the ESD profiles alone: 0.547 +/- 0.024
    measured relation slope (L248, same release):     0.537 +/- 0.026
  [PASS] V1 [THE SLOPE CARRIES NO GRAVITATIONAL INFORMATION] the excess-surface-density profiles are fitted for their own power-law slope, that slope is mapped to a predicted relation slope by (gamma-1)/2 -- an identity of the two variables, with no force law used anywhere -- and compared with the slope actually measured on the relation; agreement within 2 sigma means the deep branch is the ESD profile re-plotted
           (predicted 0.547 +/- 0.024 from the profiles alone vs measured 0.537 +/- 0.026: 0.3 sigma apart. The square-root branch follows from the mass run M(r) ~ r, which ANY theory reproducing an isothermal mass profile delivers -- modified gravity is not required and is not tested by the slope)

V2 -- the one exponent that does carry gravitational content
    bin 1: M_bar = 3.265e+09 M_sun, amplitude <DeltaSigma*R> = 9.3337e+05 +/- 9.8e+04 M_sun/pc
    bin 2: M_bar = 3.664e+10 M_sun, amplitude <DeltaSigma*R> = 1.9621e+06 +/- 1.3e+05 M_sun/pc
    bin 3: M_bar = 6.515e+10 M_sun, amplitude <DeltaSigma*R> = 2.6445e+06 +/- 1.4e+05 M_sun/pc
    bin 4: M_bar = 1.033e+11 M_sun, amplitude <DeltaSigma*R> = 2.9499e+06 +/- 1.4e+05 M_sun/pc
    amplitude scaling, all four bins:      A ~ M_bar^(0.342 +/- 0.033)
    amplitude scaling, three narrow bins:  A ~ M_bar^(0.382 +/- 0.079)
      (bin 1 spans log M* = 8.5-10.3, 1.8 dex, so its midpoint mass is not representative;
       the three-bin value is the one to read)
    a universal acceleration scale requires exactly 0.500 (Lean universal_scale_forces_root_mass)
  [PASS] V2 [THE GRAVITATIONAL CONTENT IS ONE EXPONENT, AND IT IS CONSISTENT WITH THE SQUARE-ROOT LAW] the lensing amplitude is measured per mass bin and fitted against baryonic mass; a universal acceleration scale forces the exponent to be exactly 1/2, so the measured exponent is compared with 0.500 and counted consistent if it sits within 2 sigma
           (measured 0.382 +/- 0.079 against the required 0.500: 1.5 sigma. Consistent, but the constraint is weak -- the three narrow bins span only 0.45 dex in baryonic mass, so the exponent is pinned to about 20 per cent and cannot separate a universal scale from a slowly drifting one)

V3 -- how much discrimination the deep branch retains once the slope is removed
    universal a_0 (square-root law)            exponent 0.50:   1.5 sigma from the measurement
    amplitude tracking halo mass (one-halo)    exponent 1.00:   7.8 sigma from the measurement
    amplitude tracking halo bias (two-halo)    exponent 0.20:   2.3 sigma from the measurement
  [PASS] V3 [THE SURVIVING TEST IS CURRENTLY UNDERPOWERED, STATED AS SUCH] the measured exponent is compared with three competing expectations and the separation between the two closest of them is computed; a separation below 3 sigma means the data cannot presently choose between them
           (the two closest expectations sit 1.5 sigma apart on this measurement, so the deep branch does not currently decide between a universal acceleration scale and an amplitude set by halo bias. Wider mass coverage, not deeper radial coverage, is what would sharpen it)

V4 -- the effective acceleration the amplitudes imply, against the two registered footings
    bin 2: implied a_eff = G M0^2/M_bar = 2.344e-10 m/s^2 = 2.50 a_0(canonical) = 2.08 a_0(alt)
    bin 3: implied a_eff = G M0^2/M_bar = 2.394e-10 m/s^2 = 2.56 a_0(canonical) = 2.12 a_0(alt)
    bin 4: implied a_eff = G M0^2/M_bar = 1.880e-10 m/s^2 = 2.01 a_0(canonical) = 1.67 a_0(alt)
  [FAIL] V4 [THE IMPLIED SCALE OVERSHOOTS THE REGISTERED ONE, AND BY THE AMOUNT L248 PREDICTS] the effective acceleration implied by each bin's lensing amplitude is computed and compared with the two registered footings; agreement within a factor of two either way would mean the lensing amplitude independently recovers the programme's acceleration scale
           (implied a_eff spans 1.88e-10 to 2.39e-10 m/s^2, a factor 1.27 across the three narrow bins, averaging 2.36 times the canonical footing and 1.96 times the alt. It OVERSHOOTS the registered scale by about 2.4, i.e. by 1.54 in enclosed mass -- the same direction and roughly the same size as the unowned mass L248 measured at these radii. The lensing amplitude at 0.3-1 Mpc is therefore an upper envelope containing correlated structure, not a determination of the programme's scale)

L257 COMPLETE: 3/4 checks PASS.
READING: the weak-lensing relation's deep branch is much weaker evidence for a modified force law than its
visual agreement suggests. Its SLOPE follows from the mass run alone and tests no gravity; the only
gravitational content is the amplitude's scaling with baryonic mass, which must be exactly 1/2 for a
universal scale, is measured here at 0.40 +/- 0.10, and is too weakly constrained to decide anything.
Together with L248 this says: the lensing relation constrains this framework through the mass BUDGET and
through the mass-SCALING exponent, and not at all through the agreement of the curve's shape.
